---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const updated = '22 August 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles data: tool input never leaves your browser, and what the analytics and advertising do collect.`}
  canonicalPath="/privacy-policy/"
>
  <div class="container page">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="eyebrow">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Anything you type or paste into a tool on this site stays in your browser and is
        never sent to us. A couple of tools can make a real request to a third-party
        service, but only to a public, non-personal endpoint and only when you explicitly
        press a button for that purpose — never automatically, and never through any
        server we run. See "Data you enter into tools" below for exactly which tools and
        what that sends. Separately, like most websites, we collect basic anonymous traffic
        statistics and show advertising — both are described in full below.
      </p>

      <h2>Data you enter into tools</h2>
      <p>
        Every tool on this site runs as JavaScript inside your own browser. There is no
        server-side processing, no database, and no logging of tool input on our end. When
        you paste JSON, a token, a password to hash, or any other content into a tool:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>It is processed locally by your browser and then discarded when you close or reload the page.</li>
        <li>It is never sent to us, and by default never sent to any third party either.</li>
        <li>It is never stored, logged, or retained in any form on our end.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two tools are the exception, and both make that exception obvious rather than
        hiding it: cURL Command Builder's "Send request" button fires the request you built
        directly from your browser to whatever URL you entered, and Bundle Size Checker's
        "Check size" / "Check all dependencies" buttons send the package name(s) or
        dependency list you entered to the public npm registry and to esm.sh (which bundles
        the package) so it can measure the real size. Neither goes through any server we
        run, and neither fires from just typing — only from pressing that specific button.
        A package name isn't personal data the way a password or a private document is, so
        we don't consider this a change to the commitment above, but we would rather say so
        plainly than have the absolute version of this claim be technically inaccurate.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can verify all of this independently by opening your browser's developer tools,
        going to the Network tab, and using any tool on the site.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        We use Cloudflare Web Analytics to understand which pages are used and how quickly
        they load. It is cookieless and does not fingerprint or track individuals across
        sites. It records aggregate information such as page URL, referring site, country,
        browser type, and page-load timings. It does not record anything you type.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        This site is funded by display advertising served by Google AdSense. Third-party
        vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to
        this and other websites. Google's use of advertising cookies enables it and its
        partners to serve ads based on your visit to this and other sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can opt out of personalised advertising by visiting&nbsp;
        <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" rel="nofollow noopener">Google Ads Settings</a>,
        or opt out of third-party vendors' use of cookies for personalised advertising at&nbsp;
        <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="nofollow noopener">aboutads.info</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland, you
        will be asked for consent before any personalised advertising cookies are set, and
        you can change or withdraw that consent at any time.
      </p>

      <h2>Local storage</h2>
      <p>
        The site stores a single value in your browser's local storage: your light or dark
        theme preference. It never leaves your device and is not used to identify you. If
        you have not chosen a theme, nothing is stored at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Because we do not collect personal data through the tools, there is generally no
        personal data of yours for us to access, correct, or delete. For the advertising
        cookies described above, your choices are managed through the consent controls and
        the opt-out links in that section.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially, the "last updated" date above will change. The
        commitment that we never receive or store what you type is architectural — the
        site has no server capable of receiving it — and is not something we intend to
        alter. The two narrow, explicit-action exceptions described above (which send data
        to a public third-party service, not to us) are documented on each tool's own page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-7);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
